  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 445 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe theme of the long-term forecast period will be a big swing in \ntemperatures amid continued dry weather. Record-breaking warmth will \ncome to a rapid end behind a strong cold front that will sweep \nthrough the area beginning Friday. While Friday will still bring \nwell-above-normal highs for most of the area, the strong front will \nbegin to usher in a much cooler airmass from north to south by late \nFriday as gusty northwest winds increase. The overall temperature \nswing from Friday into Friday night/Saturday morning will be quite \nsubstantial, from highs in the 80s for most on Friday to the 40s and \n50s by Saturday morning. Given very little pre-frontal moisture, \nlittle if any rainfall is expected, though an isolated shower or \nsprinkle can't be entirely ruled out in north Georgia on Friday.\n\nThe weekend then brings the first bona fide Fall-like temperatures \nof the season. After the aforementioned lows in the 40s and 50s \nSaturday morning, highs Saturday will remain in the 60s and low 70s. \nSunday still looks to bring the chilliest morning with widespread \n40s across the area and even a few upper 30s possible in the \nmountains. A warming trend will then be underway by early next week \nas the surface high slides eastward into the west Atlantic and \nmidlevel ridging returns.",
